WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:05.940
Cool.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.160
So thanks so much,
everyone for coming.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.840
I think you have a
pretty cool course here.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.680
And thanks, of course, Percy,
for inviting me to give a talk.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:19.560
So I think in this class, you're
mostly talking about training

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.960
and how to train these language
models, and get to this place

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.240
where we can have these
things that can talk back to.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.360
And today, I'm going
to talk a little bit

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.360
about once you have one of
those models, what it looks

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.440
like from the other side, what
it looks like to actually serve

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:43.180
these models, do inference,
turn these things from say,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.580
electricity, into tokens,
into intelligence.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.640
And also what are some of
the fun research problems

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.200
and research things that
you can do when you look

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.480
at it from that standpoint.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.490
I'll start with some of
high level motivation.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.170
I think one thing that's become
abundantly clear to all of us

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.890
is that we are really
going through almost

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.570
a new industrial
revolution in terms

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.090
of these models and
their capabilities.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.150
These slides I made during
my job talks two years ago.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.050
So the exact examples are
going to be pretty old,

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:19.250
but you can do human-level text
generation, code generation,

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:26.170
Cursor, Claude Code,
GPT 5.5, not just 4.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.210
You can generate
images and videos

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.930
or understand images and
videos and process them.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.830
You can start to
understand new modalities.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.090
So we're starting to see
some of the applications

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.390
and the sciences in bio
and health DNA models,

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.275
all these sorts of things.

00:01:42.275 --> 00:01:43.650
One of the things
that has really

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.330
been driving my
research for a while

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.430
is the question of what has
made these advances possible,

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:52.650
and how can we improve on
them in the next generations?

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.470
And one abundant driver of these
capabilities is really scale.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.890
So again, this is
at this point, are

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:04.610
pretty old figure but these
models have really scaled up

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:05.870
in massive ways.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:09.729
So in 2018, at the beginning
of my PhD in antiquity,

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:12.010
these were hundreds
million parameter models

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:12.830
at their largest.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.670
And we were like, oh my God,
these things are pretty crazy.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.830
By 2019, we thought that they
were too dangerous to release,

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:19.970
that's GPT 2.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.570
I think in this class, you can
train a GPT 2 quality model.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:24.830
I'm not sure if you did.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.390
If you tried hard
enough, you could.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.890
And today, of course, you
have open source models

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.570
that are a trillion
parameters and more.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:38.170
Frontier is probably at 5
to 10 trillion parameters.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:39.730
It's pretty exciting.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:43.210
With these, you have a bunch
of new capabilities like chat,

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:47.170
writing code, analyzing complex
text, doing your homework

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:50.410
for you, et cetera.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:51.890
What's really
remarkable about this

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:55.680
is that this transition is
happening faster than we think.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.380
So certainly, faster than
I thought at the beginning

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:00.760
of my PhD a few years ago.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.900
So I think one really
apt analogy and the dates

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.960
actually match up in
an interesting way.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.915
So in 1902, there are 130,000
working horses in Manhattan.

00:03:10.915 --> 00:03:12.540
So these are horses
that you don't just

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.020
have around for giggles,
but you actually

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:19.580
have them because they
play some key role.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.700
And they would-- these
horses would produce--

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.820
each would produce
pounds of manure a day,

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.840
times 130,000 horses, you
have a real manure problem.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.300
So in fact, there were
entire conferences

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.660
that were just gathered
around the question

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:34.940
of-- entire academic
conferences around,

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.800
what do we do about all the
poop that these horses produce?

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.712
So in 1898, they actually
had one of these in New York.

00:03:40.712 --> 00:03:42.420
And their conclusion
from that conference

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.670
was, there's nothing we can
do about the horse manure.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.580
You just have to hold your
nose and deal with it.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:52.660
10 years later, by 1912, cars
had already outnumbered horses

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:53.530
in Manhattan.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.070
So that 10 year
transition, you saw

00:03:56.070 --> 00:04:01.790
these things that have
been around for centuries

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.750
start to be replaced by cars.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.770
And I think for us,
for language models,

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.990
for a lot of the
stuff that we do,

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.690
that 1912 moment was
probably last year.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:15.430
So at least for me,
last year, I started

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:18.810
writing the majority of my code
using these language models.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.589
Most people in my team do it.

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:23.270
I tell all my students
to do it, except when

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.230
they're doing their homework.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:28.350
But this is a really
exciting transition

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.750
that we're living through.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.070
One of these things that
is really driving this

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:38.370
is, a lot of the scale
is driven by GPUs.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:42.990
So in a very real sense, you
could say GPUs are the new oil.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.250
This is again a
older announcement,

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.470
but you're seeing hundreds
of billions of dollars

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.950
and more of
investment into GPUs.

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:55.700
History rhymes in interesting
ways with similar things,

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.920
but there are entire countries--
entire sovereign wealth funds

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:07.120
that are making these things
a major part of their piece.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.720
And one thing that is
really abundantly clear

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.660
is that inference
is really the piece.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:15.760
So you can think of inference
as the engine that turns

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.140
electricity into intelligence.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:23.040
So the same way that oil is only
useful in a car, if you can have

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.840
an engine that turns that oil
into useful kinetic motion,

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.040
inference engines,
GPU kernels, these

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.840
are the things
that really turn--

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:35.260
that really turn
these GPUs from sand,

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.560
like Percy said, into something
that we can use today.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:39.860
And I think that's clear.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:42.360
So if you think about it,
these machine learning models,

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.100
they're really just
DAGs of operations.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.360
There are some mathematical
object that exists in the ether.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.880
The inference engines, the GPU
kernels, all these pieces, these

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.880
are the things that you
actually have to program

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.620
and map them down
to ML operations.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.560
I think you do some
of that in this class.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.620
I think you implement
some flash attention.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:05:59.960
So some training side.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:01.680
But there's a whole
world of complexity

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.600
when it comes to inference and
the inference side of things.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:10.080
So if you take away
nothing from today's talk,

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.678
I hope there's one thing which
is, if you understand inference

00:06:13.678 --> 00:06:15.220
and understand the
inference engines,

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.080
if you understand
the GPU kernels

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:20.520
that underlie a lot of
the core technology,

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.960
you can enable full stack
innovation in machine learning

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:24.580
algorithms.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:27.420
So I'm going to start
with, in today's talk,

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:30.900
a high level overview of
the lifetime of a token.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.400
So when you make a request
to one of these models, what

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:36.520
happens to that
request, how it goes

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.300
through the entire
inference service,

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:41.200
what are some of the interesting
choices you have to make.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.040
And then I'll dive deeper into
two more research projects

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:50.098
around, if you take certain
pieces of this system, what

00:06:50.098 --> 00:06:51.890
are questions that you
can ask and what are

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:54.970
some things that you can do?

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:57.310
Before I get into all
the technical details,

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:58.678
I'll quickly make a plug.

00:06:58.678 --> 00:07:00.470
So I'm here representing
two organizations.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:03.930
So one is UCSD, where
I have a small lab,

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:07.150
and some of their work is
represented in this talk.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:09.210
Then I'm also
representing together

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:13.410
who did a bunch of the work
that's represented in this talk

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:14.950
together as an AI cloud.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:20.450
So there's GPUs, Inference,
Fine-Tuning, and all the rest.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:21.910
Heavy research background.

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:24.430
So including Percy, I don't
think you can see my mouse,

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:26.310
but he's on the screen.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:29.540
And then we've got a big
research presence behind that,

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:31.290
and behind a lot of
the things that you're

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:34.130
going to see in this talk.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:34.790
All right.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:37.930
I'll start by giving
a high level overview

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:39.750
of a lifetime of a token.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:43.550
So when you make a request
to a inference system,

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:46.900
what are the different pieces
that are going to go through?

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.460
And these slides are
cribbed from a presentation

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:55.340
that my student, Austin made
from his experiences together.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.020
I'll make a note that
all of the slides

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.580
are completely AI generated
from Nano Banana Pro,

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.460
but they're pretty good
as long as you don't

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.160
look too closely at the tech.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:07.160
So I'd say, at a high level
everything is correct.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:10.820
If you look very closely, you'll
see things that are very wrong.

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:16.760
This is a overview of all the
pieces of an inference engine.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.840
So I'll give a high level
description of how it works.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:22.157
So you get a request in.

00:08:22.157 --> 00:08:23.740
The first thing
that's going to happen

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:25.540
is that the request
gets scheduled

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:26.840
to various different GPUs.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.620
You might have disaggregated
prefill and decode

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:32.539
on different machines.

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:35.480
You'll run that request
against a KV Cache to see,

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:39.020
have I seen this request or
versions of this request before?

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:44.860
Is there some compute
that I can actually save?

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:49.380
Then you'll start executing
the core machine learning code.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:50.560
So here are new tokens.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:53.740
Here are new operations
that we need to compute.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:55.960
There are various optimizations
that you can make.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:57.620
So you can split
that computation

00:08:57.620 --> 00:08:59.080
across different machines.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:00.960
You can parallelize
across different nodes.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.220
You can parallelize it within
a node across different GPUs

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:05.840
depending on the
size of your model,

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:09.060
depending on how you can
split up, split that up.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:13.740
And I think one of the exciting
things is that, as we are--

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:16.700
as the hardware is
developing, we're

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.340
actually going to start to see
more different choices that you

00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:21.380
can make there.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:23.698
But once you make all those
choices, once you execute

00:09:23.698 --> 00:09:26.240
all the code at the end, you
get the tokens that you can say,

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:27.700
hey, Chad, what
did Percy mean when

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:30.620
he said linear
regression because I

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:33.100
skipped that day in my class?

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:38.140
And you get this whole
end to end experience.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.140
One of the things
that I think is

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.350
useful to start
thinking about is,

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.550
what do these different
workloads look like?

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:49.690
And there's quite a bit
of different things.

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:52.190
So this is one of the sites
where if you look too closely,

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:55.350
I think some of the
fives turned into S's.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.750
But one of the things that
you want to think about

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:00.390
is, when you're actually
serving production traffic,

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:02.390
it doesn't necessarily
look like-- certainly,

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:04.630
doesn't look what
the type of tokens

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:06.590
that you see during training.

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:11.110
But it also doesn't look like
if you just made up a traffic

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:12.130
workload in your head.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:15.610
So what is the type thing
that we typically see?

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:18.910
So for a particular
workload, you'll

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:21.570
see a particular distribution
of input and output tokens.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.090
So let's take a coding workload.

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:28.630
So let's say you're somebody
like Cursor, where you have

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:30.590
your whole code base is
available to the agent

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:32.250
and you're asking
questions about it,

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:33.750
typically, what
that will look like,

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:36.410
is that you'll have a
long amount of input.

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:39.013
So tens of Thousands
of input tokens.

00:10:39.013 --> 00:10:41.180
And then depending on how
you've trained your model,

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:43.740
the model might output some
amount of thinking tokens,

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:48.460
or it might output
some short input in.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:50.500
And this will change
based on workload.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:53.320
It'll change based on model.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:57.120
So a coding workload for
example will look very different

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:02.020
from a summarization workload,
or a narrative summarization.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:06.835
So if your work involves pasting
entire books into a chat window

00:11:06.835 --> 00:11:09.460
and then talking back and forth
to figure out what's happening,

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:12.400
that's going to look very
different than a standard chat

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:12.900
thing.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:14.340
So if you just go
to chat and say,

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:17.840
hey, explain to me first
order calculus or whatever,

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:21.440
that's going to have very
different workload shapes.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.220
The way that we use
language models today,

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:27.240
there's very turn-based
agentic workflows.

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.880
So when you're coding, you go
back and forth with your coding

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:31.340
agent, say, hey, do this.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:32.740
No, I didn't mean that, do that.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:36.040
Your coding agents themselves
can iterate on the language

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:36.940
models quite a bit.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:41.520
So they might invoke
some tools and say,

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:45.460
grep search for something in
my data-- in my code base,

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:49.040
and then take that and go
feed it back to the model.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:51.760
I might try to do an internet
search and look up this thing

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:53.480
that my user asked me.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.720
So typically, you have
these multiple turns

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.000
in your conversation.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:00.480
Now, another
interesting piece is

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:02.740
different applications
will have different times.

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:06.060
So if you're in a fast,
interactive chat based loop,

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:07.920
or if you're talking
on your phone

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:10.680
to ChatGPT in the
voice mode, you

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:13.580
might have relatively
quick responses.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:17.520
If on the other hand, you've
put together an agentic workflow

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:19.700
where you say, hey,
go do this for me,

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:22.860
I'm going to leave you alone
and just iterate on your own,

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:24.340
you'll have a different cadence.

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:27.880
If at some point your agent
gets stuck and say, hey, help,

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:30.580
I need to ask for advice,
and you don't notice it,

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:32.920
there might be another
gap between turns.

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:35.120
So all of these
things start to define

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:36.630
what your workload looks like.

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:40.070
So how many new tokens do
you get in it every time?

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:42.330
How many tokens are
you going to generate?

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:43.930
How long is my session?

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:45.610
Am I a very sticky
user who keeps

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:47.830
going back and forth
with my Claude Agent,

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:51.490
or am I a user who's just
going to ask one question

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:54.410
and then leave and
come back the next day?

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:57.930
And of course, how long
between turns do you have?

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:00.850
I, for example, have
a chat that I've

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:04.770
had with a ChatGPT agent about
how I should set up my workout

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:07.530
and my workouts week to week.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:12.210
And I interact with that
chat once every other week.

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.490
So that is a very
different traffic pattern

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:16.527
than some of the other ones.

00:13:16.527 --> 00:13:18.610
And then of course, depending
on your application,

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:19.985
you might have
different targets.

00:13:19.985 --> 00:13:23.513
So if you have a interactive
application, you might say,

00:13:23.513 --> 00:13:25.930
I want to get the first tokens
back in less than a second,

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:28.650
so that the agent can
say, hey, I'm thinking

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:31.250
and I'm going to say
blah de blah de blah.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:34.988
Or I might say, I know I'm going
to be generating 500 tokens,

00:13:34.988 --> 00:13:36.780
and I want that whole
response to come back

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:39.447
within a certain amount of time
so that my user can read it fast

00:13:39.447 --> 00:13:43.120
enough and all these pieces.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:49.980
So as that-- when
that request comes in,

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:52.800
there are a few basic
pieces that come in.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:55.820
I'll rest a bit on the
prefill and decode piece

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:56.680
in a little bit.

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:59.283
So let's say you have some
amount of text that comes in,

00:13:59.283 --> 00:14:00.700
the first thing
you're going to do

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.160
is you're going to tokenize it.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:04.140
I think you're all
familiar with that.

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:07.340
But then once there, you start
getting into a somewhat complex

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:10.220
scheduling regime because you
have to ask questions like,

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:12.320
have I seen these tokens before?

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:17.100
Can I just look up some of
the activations in a cache?

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:21.300
And then you get to basically,
these two major pieces

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:24.820
of the actual machine
learning computation.

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:26.623
These are called
prefill and decode,

00:14:26.623 --> 00:14:28.040
and they're very
different beasts.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:31.220
So prefill means let's say
you have 10,000 tokens that

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:35.175
you've never seen before, and
you want to go compute what

00:14:35.175 --> 00:14:37.050
the activations and what
the logit should be.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:39.550
So 10,000 tokens
in, one token out.

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:41.530
That's a very compute
bound operation.

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:43.670
So that actually looks
pretty close to the things

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:45.730
that you guys have been
looking at for training time.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:47.855
So when you're training,
you have some large amount

00:14:47.855 --> 00:14:48.570
of tokens.

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:50.112
You write your flash
attention kernel

00:14:50.112 --> 00:14:51.630
when you run your training loop.

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:52.810
Prefill is very similar.

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:54.658
You just don't run
your backwards pass.

00:14:54.658 --> 00:14:56.450
And then you have this
thing called decode.

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:59.190
So decode is when you're
then just generating

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:00.090
one token at a time.

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:02.010
So you pass in
these 10,000 tokens.

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:03.890
It's like, hey,
here's my code base,

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.470
tell me what function ABC does?

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:08.710
The model is going to
process the whole prompt

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:11.590
and then start generating
one token at a time, maybe

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:15.270
three or four tokens if you have
speculative decoding working.

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.830
And if you think about that
operation-- so every time you

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:20.350
generate a new token, you
then have to run that back

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:21.250
through the model.

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:23.625
If you do the math on that,
there's actually not too many

00:15:23.625 --> 00:15:25.430
flops that you need to compute.

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:29.310
So it's going to be a
relatively light computation,

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:31.450
but it's going to be very
memory bandwidth bound.

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:32.867
So what that means
is you're going

00:15:32.867 --> 00:15:34.950
to be running-- you have
to load up the model

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:37.910
every time just to
generate a single token.

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:39.342
At the end, you get--

00:15:39.342 --> 00:15:41.050
at the end of that
model pass, of course,

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:43.990
you then have a
single number that

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:47.330
represents a token that then
gets turned into a string.

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:49.930
You probably do a little
bit of processing on that.

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:51.610
So you look for stop tokens.

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:52.150
You check.

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:55.910
You might run a safety
check to say, oh,

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:58.910
is my user trying to
hack into the system

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:00.650
in a bad way, et cetera?

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:04.670
And then at the end,
you get the tokens.

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:07.830
And the inference engine is
really running in this loop,

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:10.270
waiting for these
requests to come in.

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:13.350
So it's waiting-- so it's
running this scheduling

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:17.650
execution token sampling
loop and repeating.

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:23.670
So I'll get into a
little bit of-- so now we

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:26.450
can take one step deeper
and start to look at,

00:16:26.450 --> 00:16:27.950
what does it look
like when you have

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:31.560
a system that is processing many
different requests at a time?

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:34.360
So we have this phenomenon
called-- this technique called

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:35.640
continuous batching.

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:39.080
So the way to read
this figure is that it

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:40.980
is time is flowing downward.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:44.880
So as you go down, there are
new requests that are coming in.

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:46.600
So let's say, if
we go to step one,

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:47.820
you have some long requests.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:54.920
You have a user that is asking
to analyze some long document,

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:58.400
and the engine is going and
generating a bunch of tokens

00:16:58.400 --> 00:16:58.920
out.

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:02.400
You might have another
request comes in that starts

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:04.339
to take up some resources.

00:17:04.339 --> 00:17:06.579
So these resources are
first compute resources.

00:17:06.579 --> 00:17:10.980
You have to run things over
multiple requests at once.

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:14.960
They can be memory resources if
you're filling up a KB Cache.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:18.040
And so you'll see
these multiple requests

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:20.800
happening at the same time.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:23.240
After a step, that short
request might finish

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:26.329
and you get a new
request that comes in,

00:17:26.329 --> 00:17:29.330
and maybe it's running
for a couple steps.

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:31.730
If you have another
request that comes in,

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:33.505
maybe it's another
very long request.

00:17:33.505 --> 00:17:35.630
So that's going to be that
orange one in step four.

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:37.430
But maybe you don't
have enough GPU memory.

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:40.510
So you need to store all
your KV Cache on your GPU.

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:41.630
Maybe you've run out.

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:45.450
You might start queuing
for some reason there.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.190
Then once that long
request is done,

00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:49.770
you can start the new
request, and then, et cetera,

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:51.010
you can watch it run.

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:53.010
So already, you're seeing
some of the complexity

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:57.490
when you have many different
requests living in a system

00:17:57.490 --> 00:18:02.250
and how it goes on.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:05.170
One of the pieces
that's quite important

00:18:05.170 --> 00:18:08.370
is this thing called a KV Cache.

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:12.210
So the way to think about
this is that you probably

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:15.130
have a lot of users who
are saying, hi, ChatGPT

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:16.230
or hi, Claude.

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:18.570
And then theoretically,
you don't

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:22.170
need to compute new
activations and run that again

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:24.530
for every single user.

00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:28.490
Or if you have a long book
that the user has passed in,

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:31.150
you compute, you run the
prefill over that once,

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:34.230
and then the next time you get
some addendum to that request.

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:35.750
The next turn in
the conversation,

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:38.530
you don't need to compute
the whole thing again.

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:42.370
So we have these
mechanisms called KC Caches

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:44.710
that use prefix
sharing, that say, hey,

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:46.890
I have this new set of
tokens that comes in,

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:50.570
use a very traditional data
structure like a basic tree,

00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:55.010
look at which tokens have I seen
before, which tokens are new,

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:57.650
and then basically, do a lookup
of what those activations are

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:00.930
going to look like.

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:04.310
Once you actually have the
computation on the GPUs,

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:06.330
there's various
ways to split it.

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:08.750
So I'm not sure if you
talked about this too much,

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:11.530
but let's say you have a
trillion parameter model

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:16.433
and you're running
on 280 gigabyte GPUs,

00:19:16.433 --> 00:19:17.850
you're not going
to be able to fit

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:21.317
the whole model onto each GPU.

00:19:21.317 --> 00:19:23.400
So there are various ways
that you might split it.

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:25.820
So you might take
every tensor and split

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.280
it four ways across four GPUs.

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:29.980
This is called
tensor parallelism.

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:33.500
Or today, we have a lot of
the state of the art models

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.120
or a mixture of experts models.

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:37.580
So you have these
individual experts

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:40.520
that get selectively activated
depending on different tokens.

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:42.940
You can split those
across different GPUs.

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:44.980
The choices that you
make at this point

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:46.940
will determine what
are the bottlenecks.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:52.140
How many GPUs do you
need to run your model?

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:55.060
How many sessions can you
serve at the same time?

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:58.060
Et cetera.

00:19:58.060 --> 00:20:00.060
One of the big
things that happens

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:05.000
is that we tend to split prefill
and decode onto different GPUs,

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:07.500
different sets of
machines, and that's

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:10.500
because they have very
different compute bottlenecks

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:11.840
and compute characteristics.

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:14.980
So prefill looks a lot like what
you guys do during training.

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:16.160
You just run it.

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:17.980
It's very flop heavy.

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:20.950
You can really use the
most out of your GPUs.

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:23.858
Decode on the other hand is
very memory bandwidth heavy.

00:20:23.858 --> 00:20:25.650
Because you're not
doing that much compute,

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:30.230
but you still have to
load up all the model

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:31.360
weights at the same time.

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:33.610
These things will also take
different amounts of time.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:36.990
So prefill will typically take a
lot longer than a single decode

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:37.498
step.

00:20:37.498 --> 00:20:39.790
But you're going to be running
a lot more decode steps,

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:41.930
because you will run
prefill once for a prompt,

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:46.190
you'll run decode once for
every token that you generate.

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:49.932
So a very basic optimization
that pretty much we've

00:20:49.932 --> 00:20:51.390
all started adopting
is that you'll

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:53.690
run prefill on one
set of workers,

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:55.790
decode on another
set of workers,

00:20:55.790 --> 00:20:57.470
so that you can
specialize those two

00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:00.990
computations to different
pieces of the stack.

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:03.110
And turns out, there's
a lot of innovation

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:05.722
that you can have when
you have these splits.

00:21:05.722 --> 00:21:07.430
Some things that you
might have heard of,

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:09.610
that when NVIDIA bought Grok.

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:15.530
So NVIDIA, the King of GPUs,
buys this new inference chip.

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:18.630
One of the reasons is
that the decode workload

00:21:18.630 --> 00:21:20.470
is so different from
prefill workload,

00:21:20.470 --> 00:21:22.870
that if you're looking
at code, you can

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:24.910
be using very different chips.

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:27.610
So for example, in the next
generation of hardware,

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:31.510
NVIDIA is planning on using
its GPUs for the pre-fill side,

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:34.730
using these LPU Grok
chips for the decode.

00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:37.850
You see similar
things with Cerebras.

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:40.770
So OpenAI has this compute
partnership with Cerebras.

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:43.350
It's another chip that's
much better at decode.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:45.030
There are other
companies like SambaNova

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:47.430
and so on, that are
also making bets

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.370
on various parts of this space.

00:21:52.930 --> 00:21:54.690
Here's a fun one,
an interesting one.

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:58.150
So when you actually
start deploying

00:21:58.150 --> 00:21:59.850
these inference
engines at scale,

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:02.710
so when you're starting to serve
trillions of tokens or more

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:05.690
a day, you start to get
some pretty nasty bugs.

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:07.210
So this is a set of bugs.

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:12.090
I think most of
these happened in

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:16.600
late last year with some of the
open source inference engines.

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:22.000
And basically, one of
the characteristics

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.840
of these large scale
systems is that something

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:26.400
that will work well
at a small scale

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:28.820
will inevitably start
breaking at a large scale.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:33.280
So we're talking events that
happen with 0.001% of the time

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:34.560
or less.

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:37.740
Some of these can be so NaN.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:39.920
So sometimes,
you'll have a kernel

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:41.620
that is very slightly wrong.

00:22:41.620 --> 00:22:46.040
But the conditions for
triggering it are very rare.

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:49.320
And you will start having
some of your logits

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:53.740
turn into NaNs halfway
through the computation.

00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:55.560
When that happens,
we figure it out

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:57.940
that the model starts
outputting the same token.

00:22:57.940 --> 00:22:59.880
So I think some
model, the output just

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:02.600
started saying, hi, hi,
hi, hi, after a while,

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:05.480
or start outputting
exclamation points

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:09.280
and you get caught
into these loops.

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:11.880
Another model, at
some point, someone

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:14.620
made a change to how
you're handling tool calls.

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:17.990
So tool calls are
when the model says,

00:23:17.990 --> 00:23:21.590
hey, I want to make an internet
search or something like that.

00:23:21.590 --> 00:23:24.110
So the model will actually
say, go do an internet search.

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:28.290
And then your end harness has to
then go use old fashioned code

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:30.290
to do the internet search.

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:35.610
At some point, those tool calls
stop being processed correctly

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:36.710
in one of the engines.

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:39.730
And the symptom for that the
completion lengths shot up

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:43.390
because the model would say,
hey, make an internet search,

00:23:43.390 --> 00:23:46.768
and then it would usually--

00:23:46.768 --> 00:23:48.310
when the model is
behaving correctly,

00:23:48.310 --> 00:23:50.050
it says, hey, make
an internet search.

00:23:50.050 --> 00:23:50.550
I'm done.

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:52.010
Go return to the user.

00:23:52.010 --> 00:23:53.190
Go do that, whatever.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:55.590
But in this case, it
wasn't returning correctly.

00:23:55.590 --> 00:23:57.390
So it'd say, hey, make
an internet search.

00:23:57.390 --> 00:23:58.750
Hey, make an internet search.

00:23:58.750 --> 00:24:01.167
Hey, I don't know why there's
no internet search going on.

00:24:01.167 --> 00:24:03.010
It would just get into
this very long doom

00:24:03.010 --> 00:24:07.250
loop for tens of
thousands of tokens.

00:24:07.250 --> 00:24:10.490
There's another one that--

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:13.900
this one was interesting
because it actually

00:24:13.900 --> 00:24:17.940
took out a number of insurance
providers at the same time.

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:21.380
And it actually got blamed
on a quantization issue.

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:23.980
But actually, what was happening
was a much more subtle bug

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:28.100
where the model would
just suddenly, randomly

00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:30.500
start to output
Chinese characters

00:24:30.500 --> 00:24:32.320
when it had no reason to.

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:37.820
So for some models,
that sometimes there's

00:24:37.820 --> 00:24:39.528
some speculation,
oh, they must have

00:24:39.528 --> 00:24:41.820
fine-tuned on a Chinese model
because my model suddenly

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:42.440
starts.

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:44.260
I ask it a question
in English and starts

00:24:44.260 --> 00:24:46.900
speaking back to me in Chinese.

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:50.140
Actually, what was happening is
that there was just an off by I

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:53.000
error in one of the kernels, and
it would be a very subtle bug.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:58.700
So sometimes, you would read in
some extra uninitialized memory

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:02.545
space from your GPU, run it
through retention, and then

00:25:02.545 --> 00:25:03.920
at the end of that
whole process,

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:05.600
you get a random
Chinese character.

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:07.980
Then the model will go,
why did I start suddenly

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:09.660
thinking in Chinese?

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:12.700
I must be-- the user must be
asking me a question in Chinese,

00:25:12.700 --> 00:25:15.340
and then it will just go
veer off into Chinese.

00:25:15.340 --> 00:25:17.820
So sometimes when
this happens, it's

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:19.340
because the model
has legitimately

00:25:19.340 --> 00:25:21.500
been trained to say--

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:23.160
to think in Chinese.

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:27.400
Sometimes, it can just be an off
by one bug in somebody's code.

00:25:31.780 --> 00:25:33.780
Some interesting things
that are starting

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:38.180
to happen in a lot of the more
advanced inference stacks.

00:25:38.180 --> 00:25:43.060
So a major piece of running
large production systems

00:25:43.060 --> 00:25:46.960
is that you want to have as
large of a KV Cache as possible.

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:50.660
So it is best if you
can cache requests

00:25:50.660 --> 00:25:53.140
from many different users
or from the same user

00:25:53.140 --> 00:25:56.700
across many different
sessions, and be able to run

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:59.100
as many sessions as possible.

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:03.020
You first started this by
storing your KV Cache on GPU,

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:05.000
and then you quickly
run out of GPU memory.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:08.460
So the next, you can start
storing into CPU DRAM.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:12.990
So if you're paying attention
to Jensen's keynotes,

00:26:12.990 --> 00:26:15.590
he's recently started
getting very obsessed

00:26:15.590 --> 00:26:18.030
with CPU performance.

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:22.750
One of the reasons is because
a past generation of CPUs

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:25.030
was actually really slow.

00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:28.390
And as a result, started
bottlenecking a bunch

00:26:28.390 --> 00:26:31.230
of very important workloads.

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:33.470
And as a result, you
start to pay attention

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:37.630
because if you're $500,000
machine is being bottlenecked

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:42.470
by the $1,000 CPU that you
purchased to put on top of it,

00:26:42.470 --> 00:26:44.013
that's not a great place.

00:26:44.013 --> 00:26:45.430
One of the reasons
that can happen

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:49.030
is that you might be storing
your KV Cache on CPU memory.

00:26:49.030 --> 00:26:50.630
And so you really
care about the speed

00:26:50.630 --> 00:26:53.270
of being able to read
that KV cache back.

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:57.470
Next, you might put more KV
cache onto the disk itself.

00:26:57.470 --> 00:27:01.590
And then you start caring
about SSDs and SSD space.

00:27:01.590 --> 00:27:04.790
Again, you've probably heard
these rumblings about OpenAI

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:08.280
buying up all the SSD,
all the DRAM in the world.

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:10.600
Part of the reason is
for stuff like this,

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:13.200
where you want to store as
much stuff in your KV Cache

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:15.117
as you can.

00:27:15.117 --> 00:27:17.700
And then of course, when you're
actually building your engine,

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:19.560
there's this
complicated dance of,

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:22.820
I haven't seen these
tokens in a while.

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:25.400
Maybe I'll evict it,
send it on to the CPU,

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:29.280
or send it into disk, or send
it into some other global store.

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:31.620
When I get a new request
in, I have to go look.

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:33.560
I have to go wait
for those to come in,

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:39.680
fetch them, load them up into
my thing, and then we're happy.

00:27:39.680 --> 00:27:42.340
It's kind of CPU offloading.

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:44.940
Is it like a special kind of
workload that gets offloaded?

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:47.920
For something fast, you
don't want to do this.

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:50.013
Time to hit a SSD is very long.

00:27:50.013 --> 00:27:51.180
Can you repeat the question?

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:51.680
Yes.

00:27:51.680 --> 00:27:54.900
Yeah, so the question is
so for this offloading,

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:58.480
is it a particular type of
workload that you offload?

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:00.785
So here, we actually
get to some pretty--

00:28:00.785 --> 00:28:03.160
i'm sure none of you guys have
taken an operating systems

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:03.660
class.

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:06.320
But you should take your
operating systems classes--

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:08.980
you actually get to some pretty
classic scheduling things.

00:28:08.980 --> 00:28:13.320
So you could have this
diagram except for the GPU

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:14.340
things on the right.

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:18.360
Looks exactly like a operating
systems diagram that you might

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:21.040
have seen in the
'70s or the '80s.

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:23.600
Because we used to have this
problem, where if you opened up

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:26.080
too many applications
on your computer,

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:28.240
you would run out of
CPU memory, and then

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:32.640
you'd have to put those
applications onto the disk.

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:35.080
And it is exactly
the same workload.

00:28:35.080 --> 00:28:40.520
So ideally, here,
actually on the left side,

00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:42.880
the Nano Banana
hallucinated evictions

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:44.600
for least recently
used which is actually

00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:46.880
a pretty decent heuristic.

00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:50.080
There's probably some OS paper
somewhere that says that LRU is

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:51.820
within 2x of what you can do.

00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:52.525
That's optimal.

00:28:52.525 --> 00:28:54.400
Of course, the best
thing that you want to do

00:28:54.400 --> 00:28:57.400
is, if you could predict
the future, you would know,

00:28:57.400 --> 00:29:01.940
oh, I'm about to have a request
come in of a particular kind.

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:05.370
Let me go prefetch
that memory in.

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:07.590
It might actually be possible
to predict the future.

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:09.730
So for example, when you
go into your chat app

00:29:09.730 --> 00:29:13.145
and you bring up some old
conversation from a month ago,

00:29:13.145 --> 00:29:14.770
that's a very strong
signal that you're

00:29:14.770 --> 00:29:16.910
going to go start asking
a question about it.

00:29:16.910 --> 00:29:21.490
You might then want to go
load that up onto a GPU.

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:24.750
But in a perfect world,
you predict the future.

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:28.010
If you can't predict the future,
you use various heuristics.

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:34.050
And really, it's a question
of how much traffic

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:37.250
you want to put onto
your GPU footprint.

00:29:37.250 --> 00:29:39.130
I've never talked to
anybody who wants to put

00:29:39.130 --> 00:29:41.450
less traffic onto their GPUs.

00:29:41.450 --> 00:29:45.490
So I think Modulo or
subject to those SLAs

00:29:45.490 --> 00:29:48.930
that we splashed up
at the beginning,

00:29:48.930 --> 00:29:50.990
you want to serve as
much traffic as you can.

00:29:54.010 --> 00:29:57.110
Some fun things that
are starting to happen.

00:29:57.110 --> 00:30:00.730
So in the last generation
of Blackwell GPUs,

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:04.860
NVIDIA started putting
together these new GPUs called

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:06.860
NVl72 Grace Blackwell chips.

00:30:06.860 --> 00:30:09.340
So these are 72 GPUs
that are connected

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:11.860
with really fast interconnects.

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:13.500
And so you start
to do things like,

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:17.300
how can I split my trillion
parameter model across all 72

00:30:17.300 --> 00:30:18.180
GPUs?

00:30:18.180 --> 00:30:19.940
Does this make any sense?

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:22.500
What does it buy me?

00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:23.900
What happens?

00:30:23.900 --> 00:30:26.280
How do I start to think
about fault tolerance?

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:30.820
So these things can fail a lot
for various different reasons.

00:30:30.820 --> 00:30:34.320
One reason is that the
connectors are flimsy.

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:36.400
They're made of plastic,
not made of metal.

00:30:36.400 --> 00:30:38.240
So if you like jam
the thing in too much,

00:30:38.240 --> 00:30:41.100
then your cables are going to
bend the things a little bit,

00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:45.860
and then you get really
flaky NV links, which

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:47.560
is a whole thing in itself.

00:30:47.560 --> 00:30:49.120
But what happens when--

00:30:49.120 --> 00:30:50.480
so this is the AI generation.

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:53.780
So let's put a fan into the
chips, which doesn't quite

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:55.088
make sense.

00:30:55.088 --> 00:30:57.380
One of the things that you
want to start thinking about

00:30:57.380 --> 00:31:00.480
is, if I've taken a model
split it across 64 GPUs,

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:03.020
and I'm serving production
traffic against millions

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:05.160
of users, trillions of tokens.

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:07.883
What do I do when a
single GPU goes down?

00:31:07.883 --> 00:31:09.800
Is there some way to
make that fault tolerant?

00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:14.020
And there's a lot of fun
things to think about there.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:15.660
And then, of course,
you're starting

00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:18.860
to see these models have a
million contexts or more.

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:20.920
How do you actually process it?

00:31:20.920 --> 00:31:24.460
Do you split that context
across many different GPUs?

00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:26.200
How do you do these
different things?

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:31.180
So I want to dive--

00:31:31.180 --> 00:31:33.780
just very briefly
highlight one set--

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:36.100
one example of an
optimization that you

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:39.660
can start to make
when you start looking

00:31:39.660 --> 00:31:42.940
at this whole process
from a systems level.

00:31:42.940 --> 00:31:47.140
So this is a piece of
work that we put together

00:31:47.140 --> 00:31:49.780
a few months ago called
Cache-aware Prefill-decode

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:51.220
Disaggregation.

00:31:51.220 --> 00:31:52.720
It's a very simple optimization.

00:31:52.720 --> 00:31:55.620
It's two lines of code
in the routing layer,

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:57.720
but can actually make a
pretty big difference.

00:31:57.720 --> 00:32:01.350
So the basic idea is, you
have all these requests that

00:32:01.350 --> 00:32:02.230
are coming in.

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:06.838
Most of them are going to
be turn by turn requests

00:32:06.838 --> 00:32:08.630
in a conversation,
where somebody's already

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:10.710
started a conversation.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:13.990
But let's say your average
conversation lasts 10 turns

00:32:13.990 --> 00:32:16.230
and then the user goes
away, that suggests

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:19.590
that 10% of your requests are
going to be very fresh, very

00:32:19.590 --> 00:32:20.595
new requests.

00:32:20.595 --> 00:32:22.470
So when you have a new
request that comes in,

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:24.850
that's going to be
thousands of tokens.

00:32:24.850 --> 00:32:26.430
It's going to look
very different.

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:29.090
It's going to be a lot
more expensive to compute.

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:31.670
You don't necessarily
want to put that prefill

00:32:31.670 --> 00:32:35.830
against a short conversation
where you're halfway

00:32:35.830 --> 00:32:36.810
through conversation.

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:39.010
So someone paste
in a book and says,

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:40.450
hey, talk to me about this book.

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:42.350
You don't want that
running at the same time

00:32:42.350 --> 00:32:44.490
as someone who's midway
through a conversation,

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:50.190
like, chat explain to me
why 1 plus 1 equals 2.

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:51.710
And chat is like,
1 plus 1 equals 2

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:52.990
because numbers and whatnot.

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:54.490
And you're like,
oh, I don't get it.

00:32:54.490 --> 00:32:58.040
You don't want that very
short question and answering

00:32:58.040 --> 00:33:00.720
to happen in the same
time, on the same GPUs,

00:33:00.720 --> 00:33:06.240
as the very long request.

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:09.400
So you can put together this
really simple router that

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:11.440
says, OK, if we have
a new request that

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:13.980
comes in that's very
low cache hit rate,

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:16.840
send it to one set of
GPUs so that those can all

00:33:16.840 --> 00:33:20.360
process things together and then
send all my other warm requests

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:22.488
to another set of prefill nodes.

00:33:22.488 --> 00:33:24.280
It turns out if you do
this, you can get up

00:33:24.280 --> 00:33:30.700
to 40% faster serving with these
very, very simple optimization.

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:33.080
So that's one set
of things I'd say,

00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:34.880
the way that I
would characterize

00:33:34.880 --> 00:33:37.880
where we are in
terms of the research

00:33:37.880 --> 00:33:39.860
and these techniques is
that we're very early.

00:33:39.860 --> 00:33:44.680
So this is the type of
thing that in 10, 20 years,

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:47.360
they're going to look back
on and be like, oh, why are

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:48.720
these guys talking about this?

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:51.920
Isn't this already
obvious to folks?

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:54.750
But really, it's because we're
starting to see these things,

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:56.990
running them at
production in new ways.

00:33:56.990 --> 00:34:03.530
Seeing these new bits
of traffic in new ways.

00:34:03.530 --> 00:34:04.030
All right.

00:34:04.030 --> 00:34:09.330
So that was a overview of the
inference life of a kernel.

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:11.690
Now I'm going to talk about
two interesting research

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:15.409
projects that are very much
inspired by some of the things

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:17.810
that we see when serving things.

00:34:17.810 --> 00:34:22.090
So this first one we're going to
be talking about language model

00:34:22.090 --> 00:34:24.290
decode, and how
you can make it go

00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:27.090
a lot faster with these
things called mega kernels.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:32.090
This is a collaboration
between Stanford and Together.

00:34:32.090 --> 00:34:34.429
So the fundamental challenge
when you're running Decode.

00:34:34.429 --> 00:34:36.969
So Decode is the process by,
you've processed your prompt

00:34:36.969 --> 00:34:39.290
and now you're generating
one token at a time.

00:34:39.290 --> 00:34:42.850
The fundamental
challenge is that you

00:34:42.850 --> 00:34:46.969
have to run the whole model
to generate a single token.

00:34:46.969 --> 00:34:50.770
So that means that instead of
using all this big parallelism

00:34:50.770 --> 00:34:52.810
that you get with
a GPU that you can

00:34:52.810 --> 00:34:54.850
do during pre-filler
training, you've

00:34:54.850 --> 00:34:58.770
now turned this massively
parallel system into basically

00:34:58.770 --> 00:35:03.130
a glorified memory loader.

00:35:03.130 --> 00:35:06.090
And one of the things that
makes this extra challenging

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:08.370
is that the way
that we typically

00:35:08.370 --> 00:35:10.970
write down kernels
and run a model

00:35:10.970 --> 00:35:14.210
is that we will write
down kernels and program

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:16.370
things one operation at a time.

00:35:16.370 --> 00:35:18.950
So you guys can probably
understand why this is so.

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:21.390
Kernels tend to be pretty
challenging to write.

00:35:21.390 --> 00:35:25.330
I'm sure you guys all had a lot
of fun writing flash attention.

00:35:25.330 --> 00:35:27.210
But that means that
typically, what we do is

00:35:27.210 --> 00:35:30.790
we look at all the different
operations in a language model,

00:35:30.790 --> 00:35:32.370
and we will run--

00:35:32.370 --> 00:35:36.730
we will write a single kernel
for that operation at a time.

00:35:36.730 --> 00:35:39.530
So this makes things a
lot easier to program

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:41.770
because you just have
to write your Norm

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:45.770
kernel, your matmul kernel,
your attention kernel.

00:35:45.770 --> 00:35:52.020
But you end up seeing, it ends
up introducing a lot of downtime

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:53.940
into your system.

00:35:53.940 --> 00:35:56.860
So this is an example
of what it might

00:35:56.860 --> 00:35:59.720
look like when you're running
inference across your kernel.

00:35:59.720 --> 00:36:03.500
So this is obviously a
cartoon, but the example

00:36:03.500 --> 00:36:09.020
is drawn from a particular
attention inference kernel.

00:36:09.020 --> 00:36:12.120
The way to read this is that
on the x-axis, you have time.

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:14.240
So time is flowing
from left to right.

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:18.860
On the y-axis, you have all
the different little streaming

00:36:18.860 --> 00:36:20.800
multiprocessors on your GPU.

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:23.940
So on a H100, there
are 132 of these.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:27.180
On the B200, there's
148, et cetera.

00:36:27.180 --> 00:36:29.420
The bars indicate useful work.

00:36:29.420 --> 00:36:35.020
So when there's a bar here, one
of the processors on the GPU

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:38.920
is actually doing work, and the
empty space is just waiting.

00:36:38.920 --> 00:36:40.820
So you're just waiting
for other operations

00:36:40.820 --> 00:36:44.980
to finish so that you can
have something else go on.

00:36:44.980 --> 00:36:48.670
And so basically, you
get into this position

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:51.808
where no matter how well
you try to write the kernel,

00:36:51.808 --> 00:36:53.850
you're always going to
have downtime in your GPU.

00:36:53.850 --> 00:36:55.933
So you're going to have
things like kernel launch.

00:36:55.933 --> 00:36:57.690
So a kernel lock
or kernel teardown,

00:36:57.690 --> 00:37:00.800
that's these big gaps in
the red and the yellow.

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:03.050
You're going to have these
things called tail effects.

00:37:03.050 --> 00:37:06.550
So this is just the
same way that if you

00:37:06.550 --> 00:37:09.910
have a short prompt that gets
processed with a very long

00:37:09.910 --> 00:37:11.950
prompt, this same thing
goes all the way down

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:13.810
to the basic
attention operation.

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:15.870
If you're processing
a batch of inputs

00:37:15.870 --> 00:37:18.210
and one input is very short,
one input is very long,

00:37:18.210 --> 00:37:22.390
you're going to be waiting for
the very long input to finish.

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:27.030
And then because you're running
these across multiple kernels,

00:37:27.030 --> 00:37:29.830
you will actually start to see
these gaps between kernels start

00:37:29.830 --> 00:37:32.270
to add up.

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:35.090
So one thing that we put
together to try to solve this,

00:37:35.090 --> 00:37:36.570
is thing called a Megakernels.

00:37:36.570 --> 00:37:38.950
So this is basically
saying instead

00:37:38.950 --> 00:37:41.750
of treating each operation in
the model as its own operation

00:37:41.750 --> 00:37:44.430
writing kernel for it, instead,
let's write a single kernel

00:37:44.430 --> 00:37:47.030
to cover multiple
operations at once.

00:37:47.030 --> 00:37:49.910
This is similar to the fusion
that you see in flash attention,

00:37:49.910 --> 00:37:53.910
except done more
aggressively across a larger

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:54.973
number of things.

00:37:54.973 --> 00:37:56.390
And in particular,
what it does is

00:37:56.390 --> 00:38:00.290
it turns a GPU from
a single device--

00:38:00.290 --> 00:38:02.990
a single operation is you
start thinking of the GPU

00:38:02.990 --> 00:38:05.290
as a massive distributed
system and saying,

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:07.650
OK, I have all this work
that I need to get done.

00:38:07.650 --> 00:38:09.890
Some of it has dependencies
on other stuff.

00:38:09.890 --> 00:38:12.350
So that red stuff
has some dependencies

00:38:12.350 --> 00:38:14.390
on some of the green bars.

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:15.410
How can I schedule it?

00:38:15.410 --> 00:38:20.850
How can I distribute the work
to maximize my GPU utilization?

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:26.090
If you do this just to the
attention inference kernel,

00:38:26.090 --> 00:38:27.910
you get 30% to 70% speedups.

00:38:27.910 --> 00:38:29.910
And then the nice thing
is that you can actually

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:31.710
do this to the whole model.

00:38:31.710 --> 00:38:35.010
So this is one layer
of a Llama-1B model.

00:38:35.010 --> 00:38:39.990
So here, we've basically
taken the entire layer

00:38:39.990 --> 00:38:41.563
and put it together
into one kernel.

00:38:41.563 --> 00:38:43.230
If you look at all
these different bars,

00:38:43.230 --> 00:38:46.360
you see things are overlapped
in really weird ways.

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:49.960
That's because we are now
starting to overlap a weight

00:38:49.960 --> 00:38:54.920
load from the next-- from
the layer into the attention,

00:38:54.920 --> 00:38:57.960
or starting to run
parts of reduction

00:38:57.960 --> 00:38:59.900
before the attention
operation is over.

00:39:03.280 --> 00:39:05.660
Here's one interesting example.

00:39:05.660 --> 00:39:07.700
So here's one thing
that you can do.

00:39:07.700 --> 00:39:12.300
So if you look at modern LLMs,
you have the attention layer.

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:14.060
You have the QKV projections.

00:39:14.060 --> 00:39:16.440
You're going to add
some rope scaling to it.

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:17.940
So that's these blue lines.

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:20.840
So those blue lines
are QKV plus rope.

00:39:20.840 --> 00:39:22.360
And then the orange
lines, this is

00:39:22.360 --> 00:39:24.660
the beginning of the attention.

00:39:24.660 --> 00:39:29.840
So one of the insights is that
you can start loading in your KV

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:33.720
Cache into your attention
before you're finished with QKV,

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:35.680
and particularly, during decode.

00:39:35.680 --> 00:39:37.680
So these orange bars with
some of these circles,

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:41.720
you start the KV Cache load
while the QKV plus rope is still

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:42.360
running.

00:39:42.360 --> 00:39:45.670
And then once QKV is done, then
you have your new query tokens.

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:48.530
You can run the rest of
the attention operation.

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:53.510
So basically, when you have this
fine-grained control of GPUs,

00:39:53.510 --> 00:39:58.410
you can start some operations
before others are over.

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:01.150
Here's another one.

00:40:01.150 --> 00:40:03.450
So again, the orange
is the first part

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:05.190
of the attention computation.

00:40:05.190 --> 00:40:08.610
The red is the O projection
that comes after attention.

00:40:08.610 --> 00:40:10.070
And you have your O projection.

00:40:10.070 --> 00:40:12.450
Start loading the weights
before your attention operation

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:13.110
is over.

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:21.330
We put this together in a
very relatively complex CUDA

00:40:21.330 --> 00:40:24.410
framework with basically
instruction and instruction

00:40:24.410 --> 00:40:26.650
based abstraction,
where we can implement

00:40:26.650 --> 00:40:30.210
each sub-kernel in
its own file, and then

00:40:30.210 --> 00:40:32.650
have a big virtualized
shared memory

00:40:32.650 --> 00:40:37.070
system to orchestrate the
running of these operations.

00:40:40.580 --> 00:40:43.140
And to do this, we put
together this library

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:48.420
called ThunderKittens, which
is one of these kernel writing

00:40:48.420 --> 00:40:49.080
libraries.

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:51.000
You can think of it
as almost like Triton,

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:52.740
except more low
level, a lot more

00:40:52.740 --> 00:40:56.820
fine-grained
control over things.

00:40:56.820 --> 00:41:00.460
The payoff is you get near speed
of light decoding inference.

00:41:00.460 --> 00:41:03.300
So these numbers are
actually a lot better now.

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:06.940
But the Megakernel shown
by this bar in teal,

00:41:06.940 --> 00:41:09.380
and you can see it runs
a lot faster than some

00:41:09.380 --> 00:41:11.620
of the other state
of the art engines.

00:41:11.620 --> 00:41:14.620
And on the H100,
it's achieving 72%

00:41:14.620 --> 00:41:17.940
bandwidth utilization, which
is near the speed of light

00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:18.720
on the GPU.

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:22.220
So if you just ignore all the
complexities of what we're

00:41:22.220 --> 00:41:25.020
doing here and say, how
fast can the GPU physically

00:41:25.020 --> 00:41:26.640
go to do this operation?

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:32.380
We are pretty close to 72%
of that speed of light.

00:41:32.380 --> 00:41:35.300
So one interesting bit there is
a takeaway from that section,

00:41:35.300 --> 00:41:38.020
is that, if you have very
deep control of the kernels

00:41:38.020 --> 00:41:40.460
and the understanding
of the hardware,

00:41:40.460 --> 00:41:43.900
you can start to enable very
different compute paradigms.

00:41:43.900 --> 00:41:45.620
And all these
things, you only see

00:41:45.620 --> 00:41:51.020
when you start playing with
inference at a deep level.

00:41:51.020 --> 00:41:52.900
And now, I'll talk
about something

00:41:52.900 --> 00:41:54.120
that's a little bit new.

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:58.780
So this is now getting into
the realm of new architectures.

00:41:58.780 --> 00:42:01.540
And I'm going to be talking
about this new model called

00:42:01.540 --> 00:42:02.040
Parcae.

00:42:02.040 --> 00:42:04.180
So this is some
work that comes out

00:42:04.180 --> 00:42:06.780
of my UCSD lab led
by Hayden, and then

00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:12.300
also in collaboration with these
two folks, Zachary and Taylor.

00:42:12.300 --> 00:42:13.880
So I'm going to
come back to this.

00:42:13.880 --> 00:42:16.300
So at the beginning of the
talk, I talked about, hey,

00:42:16.300 --> 00:42:17.877
all these new
capabilities are coming

00:42:17.877 --> 00:42:20.460
because you're starting to scale
these models, and parameters,

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:21.252
and data.

00:42:21.252 --> 00:42:23.460
With Parcae, we wanted to
ask another question, which

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:26.220
is, is this the only
way you have to scale?

00:42:26.220 --> 00:42:28.460
Or is it potentially is
there potentially something

00:42:28.460 --> 00:42:32.660
else-- some other way that
you can get this quality?

00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:35.820
And with Parcae, we wanted
to-- this was basically

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:38.930
our take on a technique
called loop transformers,

00:42:38.930 --> 00:42:41.190
where you take some
blocks of your transformer

00:42:41.190 --> 00:42:42.570
and you run them in a loop.

00:42:42.570 --> 00:42:46.673
So instead of having
your tokens go all one

00:42:46.673 --> 00:42:48.090
layer at a time
through the model,

00:42:48.090 --> 00:42:51.010
at some point you say, hey,
as you're going through,

00:42:51.010 --> 00:42:54.750
just send it back through
that loop at a time.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:57.250
You're going to see, there's a
couple different pieces here.

00:42:57.250 --> 00:43:00.870
So one is, we use some State
Space Model theory, some SSM

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:03.810
theory to stabilize
this operation.

00:43:03.810 --> 00:43:07.110
Naively, if you just run
this and let the thing train,

00:43:07.110 --> 00:43:09.150
we saw that the thing
was going to blow up.

00:43:09.150 --> 00:43:10.790
And then the other
thing is we started

00:43:10.790 --> 00:43:14.030
to see some interesting scaling
laws that suggest that you want

00:43:14.030 --> 00:43:17.470
to be scaling the recurrence
of these recurrent models

00:43:17.470 --> 00:43:19.790
as you increase the data.

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:24.890
So in order to make the best
use out of your parameters,

00:43:24.890 --> 00:43:29.030
you want to actually be
reusing them somewhat.

00:43:29.030 --> 00:43:31.270
First, I'll talk a
little bit of motivation

00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:35.120
of why we found this looping
problem to be very interesting.

00:43:35.120 --> 00:43:39.280
So the basic idea is, let's say
you have some activation that's

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:41.280
going through part of
your model, at some point,

00:43:41.280 --> 00:43:43.800
it hits these loops
blocks and it's just going

00:43:43.800 --> 00:43:45.340
to run the same activation.

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:48.460
It's going to run through that
same layer some number of times.

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:51.400
So that purple block
is the recurrent block.

00:43:51.400 --> 00:43:55.440
And then at the end, you get
the thing that comes back.

00:43:55.440 --> 00:43:57.980
So there are some
advantages of this.

00:43:57.980 --> 00:43:59.700
So you can keep your
parameters constant,

00:43:59.700 --> 00:44:02.440
but it gives you a dial
to increase your flops.

00:44:02.440 --> 00:44:06.240
So if you think that
flops-- more flops

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:08.080
equals higher
quality, this is a way

00:44:08.080 --> 00:44:12.040
to increase your quality without
paying a higher parameter cost.

00:44:12.040 --> 00:44:15.080
Another thing is,
there's this old work--

00:44:15.080 --> 00:44:17.360
old is a relative term
from a few years ago--

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:19.860
that suggests that you actually
get higher expressivity--

00:44:19.860 --> 00:44:21.060
their expressivity.

00:44:21.060 --> 00:44:26.040
There are things that
you can't express

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:27.880
with the same number
of parameters,

00:44:27.880 --> 00:44:32.560
that you can express
with these looped models.

00:44:32.560 --> 00:44:34.720
And one of our driving
questions was, what's

00:44:34.720 --> 00:44:36.288
the best quality per parameter?

00:44:36.288 --> 00:44:38.080
What's the best
intelligence per parameter?

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:40.320
Or intelligence per
parameter and data

00:44:40.320 --> 00:44:44.640
that these things
will allow you to do?

00:44:44.640 --> 00:44:46.820
There are some promising
initial results.

00:44:46.820 --> 00:44:51.357
So this is a paper from Tom
Goldstein's group in Maryland

00:44:51.357 --> 00:44:53.440
that suggests that hey,
this thing might be better

00:44:53.440 --> 00:44:55.320
than transformers.

00:44:55.320 --> 00:44:59.852
These are some results
on the Arc tasks.

00:44:59.852 --> 00:45:01.560
And there's also a
bunch of Twitter hype,

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:04.760
because about a week
before we released Parcae,

00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:08.120
some dude from OpenAI said that
Claude mythos is a recurrent--

00:45:08.120 --> 00:45:09.920
is a looped language model.

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:11.005
I don't think it's right.

00:45:11.005 --> 00:45:12.380
I think he was
just making it up.

00:45:12.380 --> 00:45:17.640
But as a result, it
really blew up in Twitter

00:45:17.640 --> 00:45:21.122
in speculation
right before this.

00:45:21.122 --> 00:45:23.580
Eventually, he had to write
this blog post being like, hey,

00:45:23.580 --> 00:45:25.080
my bad, I just made that up.

00:45:25.080 --> 00:45:26.920
None of it's true.

00:45:26.920 --> 00:45:29.020
But it was sufficiently
interesting to go.

00:45:29.020 --> 00:45:31.890
So we were working on it
before this Twitter hype,

00:45:31.890 --> 00:45:35.690
but I think there was something
interesting, something

00:45:35.690 --> 00:45:38.250
interesting to look at here.

00:45:38.250 --> 00:45:40.410
Now, one problem with
these looped models

00:45:40.410 --> 00:45:43.270
is if you looked at any of them
and you tried to train them,

00:45:43.270 --> 00:45:46.010
and then you changed anything
about the training algorithm

00:45:46.010 --> 00:45:46.510
at all.

00:45:46.510 --> 00:45:50.030
So if you change the learning
rate by a little bit,

00:45:50.030 --> 00:45:52.350
you'd suddenly start to
see these models blow up.

00:45:52.350 --> 00:45:54.910
So if you did a simple thing
like a learning rate sweep,

00:45:54.910 --> 00:45:57.970
you'd see nine times out
of 10, this model just

00:45:57.970 --> 00:46:00.030
isn't going to converge,
it's going to blow up.

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:01.072
You're going to get NaNs.

00:46:01.072 --> 00:46:03.050
You're going to get
these big loss spikes.

00:46:03.050 --> 00:46:04.810
And so we were
saying, hey, there

00:46:04.810 --> 00:46:06.393
seems to be something
wrong with this.

00:46:06.393 --> 00:46:08.893
So if you're training a model
and you're actually scaling it

00:46:08.893 --> 00:46:11.130
up, and you see there,
these big loss spikes, that

00:46:11.130 --> 00:46:13.610
suggests something has gone
very wrong with your training

00:46:13.610 --> 00:46:15.970
process, you should
take a deeper look

00:46:15.970 --> 00:46:18.465
and try to figure
out what happened.

00:46:18.465 --> 00:46:20.090
In previous work,
there were some hacks

00:46:20.090 --> 00:46:22.010
like you can put
norms in every layer

00:46:22.010 --> 00:46:25.250
to figure out what's
happening or just pick two--

00:46:25.250 --> 00:46:27.190
the learning rate of 2e-4.

00:46:27.190 --> 00:46:29.360
Don't pick any of the
other learning rates.

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:32.500
But we were thinking, hey, the
existence of these loss spikes

00:46:32.500 --> 00:46:39.140
suggests that there's probably
something more deeper going on.

00:46:39.140 --> 00:46:42.300
And so we took a bit of a
mathematical state space

00:46:42.300 --> 00:46:47.500
model-esque approach to
the stabilization question.

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:52.620
Our basic insight was, you
can look at this process.

00:46:52.620 --> 00:46:54.560
So actually, if you
look at this process,

00:46:54.560 --> 00:46:58.692
you try to think about how
to analyze it analytically.

00:46:58.692 --> 00:47:00.900
It's going to be very
complicated, because this big R

00:47:00.900 --> 00:47:03.260
block has tons of parameters.

00:47:03.260 --> 00:47:05.040
There's all sorts
of nonlinearities.

00:47:05.040 --> 00:47:08.420
There's a Softmax,
there's GELU, and RoPE,

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:09.680
and all these other things.

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:12.320
So if you try to
analyze it analytically,

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:14.180
it's quite complex.

00:47:14.180 --> 00:47:16.160
Our insight was, we
were just saying,

00:47:16.160 --> 00:47:19.620
OK, let's actually just look
at the residual of this thing.

00:47:19.620 --> 00:47:23.980
So how is this activation
changing from block to block?

00:47:23.980 --> 00:47:25.758
Our first empirical
observation was hey,

00:47:25.758 --> 00:47:27.300
it actually doesn't
change that much.

00:47:27.300 --> 00:47:30.500
So each of these
residual blocks is

00:47:30.500 --> 00:47:32.040
changing the vector
a little bit,

00:47:32.040 --> 00:47:37.940
but not really having
a massive impact.

00:47:37.940 --> 00:47:41.100
And as a result, how
can we-- so maybe

00:47:41.100 --> 00:47:42.280
we can actually model this.

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:46.340
Maybe we can actually look at
what's happening more deeply.

00:47:46.340 --> 00:47:49.700
And so what we did is we went
and wrote down a dynamic system

00:47:49.700 --> 00:47:51.180
over this residual.

00:47:51.180 --> 00:47:53.820
And when we did, we realized
there's a couple pieces in here

00:47:53.820 --> 00:47:57.660
that when you look at how
you write down the model,

00:47:57.660 --> 00:48:02.140
might look relatively
benign, but actually

00:48:02.140 --> 00:48:04.060
end up being a huge bit.

00:48:04.060 --> 00:48:06.980
So what we did was we said,
OK, there's all this nonlinear

00:48:06.980 --> 00:48:11.060
stuff, there's this
attention, there's this GELU,

00:48:11.060 --> 00:48:12.700
there's this big
feed-forward network

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:16.420
with the intermediates
and stuff,

00:48:16.420 --> 00:48:18.080
we're going to all
put that into a box.

00:48:18.080 --> 00:48:20.460
We're just going to
call that R. And this R

00:48:20.460 --> 00:48:23.020
is going to be some
big nonlinear thing.

00:48:23.020 --> 00:48:29.270
But we're going to put it--
just take it to the side.

00:48:29.270 --> 00:48:32.850
What you're left with is
these A and B matrices.

00:48:32.850 --> 00:48:37.630
So this B matrix is
some transformation

00:48:37.630 --> 00:48:40.450
over your initial vector.

00:48:40.450 --> 00:48:43.790
So what is that first vector
before you start the loop?

00:48:43.790 --> 00:48:47.470
And then this A vector is-- this
A matrix is how do you transform

00:48:47.470 --> 00:48:50.630
that residual in each loop?

00:48:50.630 --> 00:48:53.670
And what we did is we said, hey,
this simple thing, if you take

00:48:53.670 --> 00:48:56.190
all the complexity
of the transformer,

00:48:56.190 --> 00:48:59.110
stick it to the side, you
have a relatively simple way

00:48:59.110 --> 00:49:01.750
of looking at all the
previous loop transformers.

00:49:01.750 --> 00:49:04.070
And in previous
cases, you do end up

00:49:04.070 --> 00:49:05.970
making some pretty
normal decisions.

00:49:05.970 --> 00:49:10.590
So in one case you
just say, oh, you just

00:49:10.590 --> 00:49:11.897
treat it as the identity.

00:49:11.897 --> 00:49:13.230
You're just going to add things.

00:49:13.230 --> 00:49:18.150
In another case, it's a
fully learnable matrix.

00:49:18.150 --> 00:49:21.550
And then we said, OK, what
happens if we just so--

00:49:21.550 --> 00:49:24.800
the residual-- we
observed empirically,

00:49:24.800 --> 00:49:28.400
that these A and B matrices
are actually dominating

00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:30.880
the magnitude of this equation.

00:49:30.880 --> 00:49:34.280
What happens if you just drop
that complicated nonlinear

00:49:34.280 --> 00:49:34.820
piece?

00:49:34.820 --> 00:49:35.820
What do you get out?

00:49:35.820 --> 00:49:38.320
Well, you get out actually
a pretty simple system

00:49:38.320 --> 00:49:40.140
that if you use high
school calculus,

00:49:40.140 --> 00:49:42.000
you can just solve
it to figure out what

00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:44.200
the answers are going to be.

00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:46.240
So here's the closed
form solution.

00:49:46.240 --> 00:49:48.520
You can actually
compute what is,

00:49:48.520 --> 00:49:50.320
given what the
initial activation is,

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:52.500
and what that
initial injection is,

00:49:52.500 --> 00:49:54.920
you can actually just
empirically compute

00:49:54.920 --> 00:50:00.280
what the activation is going
to look like at Step T plus 1.

00:50:00.280 --> 00:50:01.940
And you notice a
couple of things.

00:50:01.940 --> 00:50:08.040
So this thing is dominated
by these A matrices.

00:50:08.040 --> 00:50:11.160
And especially, this A matrix
that you are powering up

00:50:11.160 --> 00:50:14.600
to a large degree.

00:50:14.600 --> 00:50:17.600
And from here,
what we realize is

00:50:17.600 --> 00:50:20.080
that there's this quantity
called the spectral radius

00:50:20.080 --> 00:50:22.610
of this A. So spectral
radius is basically

00:50:22.610 --> 00:50:24.675
like another word for norm.

00:50:24.675 --> 00:50:26.050
One way that you
can look at this

00:50:26.050 --> 00:50:29.630
is that, you're
taking this matrix.

00:50:29.630 --> 00:50:32.770
You're powering it
up to huge amounts.

00:50:32.770 --> 00:50:36.010
If this matrix can learn
to be something like--

00:50:36.010 --> 00:50:38.330
let's say that this matrix--

00:50:38.330 --> 00:50:40.890
if you go to scalars, imagine
that this matrix is 2.

00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:43.470
And then this T is
like 16 or something,

00:50:43.470 --> 00:50:44.910
you've now taken
this activation,

00:50:44.910 --> 00:50:47.370
you've blown it up to
2 to the power of 16.

00:50:47.370 --> 00:50:48.970
And it's really big.

00:50:48.970 --> 00:50:52.490
And this starts to explain
some of those big loss spikes.

00:50:52.490 --> 00:50:55.130
And in particular, what we found
is that, the choices that you

00:50:55.130 --> 00:50:59.050
make for these A and
B matrices that people

00:50:59.050 --> 00:51:01.690
have made in these previous
papers are either--

00:51:01.690 --> 00:51:05.370
we call them, marginally
stable or unstable.

00:51:05.370 --> 00:51:08.390
Unstable-- make the
system unstable.

00:51:10.970 --> 00:51:13.090
And so with Parcae
we said, OK, well, we

00:51:13.090 --> 00:51:16.410
figured out this thing where if
you take this particular look

00:51:16.410 --> 00:51:19.992
at it, and you let these A
and B do whatever they want,

00:51:19.992 --> 00:51:21.950
you're going to get things
that really explode.

00:51:21.950 --> 00:51:25.610
What if we just constrain A and
B such that if you run the math

00:51:25.610 --> 00:51:27.290
they're not going to explode?

00:51:27.290 --> 00:51:29.950
So for A, what we
did is we said,

00:51:29.950 --> 00:51:31.970
OK, we're effectively
going to make

00:51:31.970 --> 00:51:34.190
that A matrix a negative
diagonal matrix.

00:51:34.190 --> 00:51:37.910
So if you power that up, the
term eventually goes to zero,

00:51:37.910 --> 00:51:39.675
so it doesn't blow up.

00:51:39.675 --> 00:51:41.050
This B matrix,
we're going to put

00:51:41.050 --> 00:51:43.730
a-- stick a really simple
linear norm against it,

00:51:43.730 --> 00:51:46.330
because this B matrix actually,
only gets applied once

00:51:46.330 --> 00:51:48.570
and doesn't really blow up.

00:51:48.570 --> 00:51:49.930
E can be the spectral radius.

00:51:49.930 --> 00:51:51.305
It's now going to
be less than 1.

00:51:51.305 --> 00:51:55.130
It's now actually going
to be a stable system.

00:51:55.130 --> 00:51:57.930
And now if you go train
this model, what we saw

00:51:57.930 --> 00:52:00.270
is that you actually
saw stable loss curve.

00:52:00.270 --> 00:52:03.870
So Parcae is this stabilized
thing where we re-parameterize

00:52:03.870 --> 00:52:08.570
A and B. You can see that even
with this the 6e-4 learning rate

00:52:08.570 --> 00:52:11.030
that was so bad for
the other models,

00:52:11.030 --> 00:52:13.890
you actually got a
stable model at the end.

00:52:13.890 --> 00:52:16.490
And you see that
with doing this,

00:52:16.490 --> 00:52:22.420
you can naturally constrain the
state norm of the activations.

00:52:22.420 --> 00:52:23.560
So two pieces here.

00:52:23.560 --> 00:52:26.260
So that orange baseline is
just a completely unconstrained

00:52:26.260 --> 00:52:26.760
model.

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:29.700
You can see it blows up,
goes to 10 to the 19th.

00:52:29.700 --> 00:52:34.740
This blue line is
actually a model where

00:52:34.740 --> 00:52:37.100
you are applying a norm to it.

00:52:37.100 --> 00:52:39.860
So what happens here is
that the model is actually

00:52:39.860 --> 00:52:44.000
trying to expand the
activations because it's saying,

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:47.740
oh, with more room, I can
represent different things

00:52:47.740 --> 00:52:48.240
better.

00:52:48.240 --> 00:52:50.540
I can put these different
concepts further away

00:52:50.540 --> 00:52:52.482
from each other or whatnot.

00:52:52.482 --> 00:52:53.940
And then you're
applying norm to it

00:52:53.940 --> 00:52:56.120
to take that big thing
that's trying to expand.

00:52:56.120 --> 00:52:59.140
Then you try to norm
it back down to one.

00:52:59.140 --> 00:53:01.180
And then you have this big--

00:53:01.180 --> 00:53:04.220
these two pressures that are
fighting against each other that

00:53:04.220 --> 00:53:05.640
manifests in loss spikes.

00:53:05.640 --> 00:53:08.160
So even though on the right,
your norms are very good,

00:53:08.160 --> 00:53:10.820
you're not seeing the
activation actually blow up.

00:53:10.820 --> 00:53:16.230
You do see that the loss can
do some pretty gnarly things.

00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:19.350
So basically, there's
this pretty simple change

00:53:19.350 --> 00:53:22.610
in the activations can
stabilize the training,

00:53:22.610 --> 00:53:26.590
stabilize these recurrent loops.

00:53:26.590 --> 00:53:28.370
So it's not only a
more stable system,

00:53:28.370 --> 00:53:31.550
you actually also get more
higher quality models.

00:53:31.550 --> 00:53:36.710
So this is a table where we
are comparing Parcae models

00:53:36.710 --> 00:53:39.790
against a previous loop
transformer called Recurrent

00:53:39.790 --> 00:53:40.490
Depth Model.

00:53:40.490 --> 00:53:42.190
So you can see
higher performance

00:53:42.190 --> 00:53:45.430
across a variety
of applications.

00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:48.790
So you see Parcae outperforms
the previous models

00:53:48.790 --> 00:53:51.250
and also outperforms strong
transformer baseline.

00:53:51.250 --> 00:53:54.550
So this transformer is one
of the nanochannel ones where

00:53:54.550 --> 00:53:56.590
a bunch of people are
trying to just get

00:53:56.590 --> 00:53:58.870
it to learn as fast as it can.

00:53:58.870 --> 00:54:01.550
And these Parcae models, if you
take that same basic transformer

00:54:01.550 --> 00:54:04.890
architecture, start looping
it, and then stabilize it,

00:54:04.890 --> 00:54:08.950
you get better
perplexities, better end

00:54:08.950 --> 00:54:10.490
to end quality as well.

00:54:13.030 --> 00:54:14.790
And then one-- and
then we started

00:54:14.790 --> 00:54:17.830
to run some very basic
scaling laws here.

00:54:17.830 --> 00:54:20.790
And here, our question was, OK,
this is really, really cute.

00:54:20.790 --> 00:54:23.210
You can do this looping
thing to make things better.

00:54:23.210 --> 00:54:25.510
But really, should you--
is there any evidence

00:54:25.510 --> 00:54:27.150
that there's
something here that we

00:54:27.150 --> 00:54:32.590
want to be doing looping
more aggressively?

00:54:32.590 --> 00:54:34.710
So here, I'll take a step back.

00:54:34.710 --> 00:54:37.310
A few years ago, there
was all this work

00:54:37.310 --> 00:54:41.030
as we started to enter this
regime where you wanted

00:54:41.030 --> 00:54:43.590
to scale up all these
models, a natural question

00:54:43.590 --> 00:54:45.470
is, should I make
the models bigger

00:54:45.470 --> 00:54:47.950
or should I just
train on more data?

00:54:47.950 --> 00:54:49.790
So a few years
ago, folks started

00:54:49.790 --> 00:54:53.037
to ask, how should I scale these
two quantities with each other?

00:54:53.037 --> 00:54:54.370
Should I scale model parameters?

00:54:54.370 --> 00:54:55.910
Should I scale data?

00:54:55.910 --> 00:54:59.750
And we came up with all these
very complicated power law

00:54:59.750 --> 00:55:01.497
curves that all look like this.

00:55:01.497 --> 00:55:02.330
They're very pretty.

00:55:02.330 --> 00:55:03.570
There's lots of colors.

00:55:03.570 --> 00:55:07.910
What you want to look
out for is basically--

00:55:07.910 --> 00:55:10.790
what you want to look out for
is, if that curve is going down

00:55:10.790 --> 00:55:12.400
into the right, it
suggests you should

00:55:12.400 --> 00:55:16.140
be scaling both data and
parameters at the same time.

00:55:16.140 --> 00:55:17.640
If we were going
straight down, that

00:55:17.640 --> 00:55:20.620
would mean just increase
your training data.

00:55:20.620 --> 00:55:23.958
There's no need to
increase your parameters.

00:55:23.958 --> 00:55:26.000
If it's going-- if it's
flat, just going straight

00:55:26.000 --> 00:55:29.660
to the right, that means don't
increase your data at all,

00:55:29.660 --> 00:55:31.347
just increase your parameters.

00:55:31.347 --> 00:55:33.180
But you see, it's going
down into the right.

00:55:33.180 --> 00:55:36.385
That means you should scale data
and parameters at the same time.

00:55:36.385 --> 00:55:37.260
And now you see this.

00:55:37.260 --> 00:55:38.840
You train a $1
trillion parameter

00:55:38.840 --> 00:55:44.280
model on $35 trillion tokens,
and you get better quality.

00:55:44.280 --> 00:55:48.200
So our question was, OK, where
does recurrence fit into this?

00:55:48.200 --> 00:55:50.180
And there are a couple--

00:55:50.180 --> 00:55:52.980
so down into the right means you
want to scale data in params--

00:55:52.980 --> 00:55:57.760
there are a couple
possibilities with recurrence.

00:55:57.760 --> 00:56:00.640
And so for example,
you might conclude,

00:56:00.640 --> 00:56:02.260
you should never
run a recurrence.

00:56:02.260 --> 00:56:05.380
It's better to just keep the
same single recurrent model.

00:56:05.380 --> 00:56:07.860
You might conclude, you
should do a ton of recurrence,

00:56:07.860 --> 00:56:12.970
or maybe only some
recurrence to the bit.

00:56:12.970 --> 00:56:16.250
What we're showing here is, at
least in these initial scaling

00:56:16.250 --> 00:56:20.010
laws, all of these
curves are isometric,

00:56:20.010 --> 00:56:21.630
and ISO-param, ISO-FLOP.

00:56:21.630 --> 00:56:23.955
So each of these
curves is a model.

00:56:23.955 --> 00:56:25.330
So on the left
and the right, you

00:56:25.330 --> 00:56:27.650
have the same number
of parameters.

00:56:27.650 --> 00:56:29.828
As you go down, as
you change the colors,

00:56:29.828 --> 00:56:31.370
we are increasing
the amount of flops

00:56:31.370 --> 00:56:34.570
that you use to train
the model by increasing

00:56:34.570 --> 00:56:35.730
the amount of data.

00:56:35.730 --> 00:56:38.290
So here, we're varying
data and varying

00:56:38.290 --> 00:56:40.650
the number of recurrences.

00:56:40.650 --> 00:56:43.010
What we find is that in
both of these models,

00:56:43.010 --> 00:56:45.130
you see this down into
the right trend again.

00:56:45.130 --> 00:56:49.290
So what this is suggesting is
that for these fixed parameter

00:56:49.290 --> 00:56:52.150
training things, as you
increase the amount of data,

00:56:52.150 --> 00:56:54.770
you should actually also
be increasing the amount

00:56:54.770 --> 00:57:00.290
of recurrences that you have.

00:57:00.290 --> 00:57:03.690
We find that these recurrences
follow some pretty classic power

00:57:03.690 --> 00:57:04.810
laws.

00:57:04.810 --> 00:57:07.650
So you can actually
start to predict.

00:57:07.650 --> 00:57:11.130
You can get these scaling laws
to start to predict quality,

00:57:11.130 --> 00:57:15.230
as you scale, recurrences
and your tokens jointly.

00:57:15.230 --> 00:57:15.730
Yeah.

00:57:15.730 --> 00:57:18.650
For a fixed model size
[INAUDIBLE] recurrence.

00:57:18.650 --> 00:57:21.110
What about jointly [INAUDIBLE]
scaling [INAUDIBLE]?

00:57:21.110 --> 00:57:21.610
Yeah.

00:57:21.610 --> 00:57:26.730
So we have this really
complex 3D figure that showed

00:57:26.730 --> 00:57:30.610
recurrences, and
data, and parameters.

00:57:30.610 --> 00:57:35.650
And it pointed also whatever
down into the right,

00:57:35.650 --> 00:57:39.250
and down that way
at the same time.

00:57:39.250 --> 00:57:41.970
So if you believe
that figure, it

00:57:41.970 --> 00:57:44.930
suggests you should be
scaling all three together.

00:57:44.930 --> 00:57:47.810
But that figure was just really
hard to look at, because it was

00:57:47.810 --> 00:57:50.430
3D and weird.

00:57:50.430 --> 00:57:54.630
So these power laws suggest,
when you're increasing data

00:57:54.630 --> 00:57:56.930
you should be
increasing recurrence.

00:57:56.930 --> 00:57:59.423
And then you have other
power laws that suggest,

00:57:59.423 --> 00:58:02.090
when you're increasing data you
should be increasing parameters.

00:58:02.090 --> 00:58:04.853
So obviously, you
should increase--

00:58:04.853 --> 00:58:07.020
well, it suggests that you
should increase all three

00:58:07.020 --> 00:58:09.100
of them if you can.

00:58:09.100 --> 00:58:11.740
But one piece here
is that if you're

00:58:11.740 --> 00:58:13.380
going to fix your
model size and you're

00:58:13.380 --> 00:58:15.180
going to increase
the amount of data,

00:58:15.180 --> 00:58:18.340
you should also be increasing
the recurrence, which

00:58:18.340 --> 00:58:21.580
is interesting because as far
as I know, all of our models

00:58:21.580 --> 00:58:23.080
today have no
recurrence in them.

00:58:23.080 --> 00:58:26.980
So they're all at the
very left of these curves.

00:58:26.980 --> 00:58:28.770
And they all have a
ton of data, which

00:58:28.770 --> 00:58:31.020
suggests that there might
be something slightly better

00:58:31.020 --> 00:58:36.180
that we could be doing
when training these models.

00:58:36.180 --> 00:58:40.240
And here's just that
experiment in another way.

00:58:40.240 --> 00:58:43.940
So on the orange curve,
you have-- so here,

00:58:43.940 --> 00:58:46.680
we fixed the model size.

00:58:46.680 --> 00:58:49.920
The orange curve is
a fixed depth model.

00:58:49.920 --> 00:58:52.200
So this is like a traditional
transformer model.

00:58:52.200 --> 00:58:57.900
The blue curve is when
you fix the flop budget,

00:58:57.900 --> 00:59:02.420
where do you hit on the
curve for a looping model?

00:59:02.420 --> 00:59:06.170
So here, the orange
and blue dots

00:59:06.170 --> 00:59:08.170
have been trained with
the same number of flops,

00:59:08.170 --> 00:59:11.590
but a different amount of data,
but they're the same size.

00:59:11.590 --> 00:59:13.830
When you get to
that number of flops

00:59:13.830 --> 00:59:16.990
by increasing
recurrences as well as--

00:59:16.990 --> 00:59:18.470
and not just
increasing data, you

00:59:18.470 --> 00:59:22.830
start to get smaller validation
losses, which again suggests

00:59:22.830 --> 00:59:26.270
it might be the case that
we should be looping all

00:59:26.270 --> 00:59:27.770
of our big pre-training runs.

00:59:30.910 --> 00:59:32.630
So that's Parcae.

00:59:32.630 --> 00:59:36.550
If I take a little
step back and go back

00:59:36.550 --> 00:59:40.110
to the big takeaway of the
talk, I think hopefully,

00:59:40.110 --> 00:59:41.910
I've given you a
little sense today

00:59:41.910 --> 00:59:44.450
of if you understand the
inference of these models,

00:59:44.450 --> 00:59:46.350
if you understand
the GPU kernels,

00:59:46.350 --> 00:59:50.290
you understand all the
pieces that goes into it.

00:59:50.290 --> 00:59:53.077
You can really start to
enable full stack innovation

00:59:53.077 --> 00:59:54.410
and machine learning algorithms.

00:59:54.410 --> 00:59:57.110
So whether that's through a
new routing algorithm that

00:59:57.110 --> 00:59:59.630
lets you serve
more traffic serve

00:59:59.630 --> 01:00:00.970
traffic in a different way.

01:00:00.970 --> 01:00:03.960
Or new kernels that allow you
to run part of your system way

01:00:03.960 --> 01:00:04.740
faster.

01:00:04.740 --> 01:00:07.800
Or new architectures
that say, if you

01:00:07.800 --> 01:00:10.120
have a lot fewer
parameters, you can fit them

01:00:10.120 --> 01:00:12.740
in a subset of your
GPUs in a different way,

01:00:12.740 --> 01:00:15.120
reduce the amount
of communication,

01:00:15.120 --> 01:00:18.600
these are all different
pieces of that question

01:00:18.600 --> 01:00:23.520
of that research problem
that we're seeing today.

01:00:23.520 --> 01:00:26.520
And yeah, hopefully I've
inspired at least one of you

01:00:26.520 --> 01:00:31.120
in this room to go take a
deeper look at some of that.

01:00:31.120 --> 01:00:32.340
So with that, thanks.

01:00:32.340 --> 01:00:33.820
Happy to take a few questions.

01:00:33.820 --> 01:00:35.775
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:37.720 --> 01:00:41.100
We have about five, 10
minutes for questions.

01:00:41.100 --> 01:00:43.020
So if you have a
question, raise your hand.

01:00:46.000 --> 01:00:50.460
Parcae, are you training
from scratch on the loop?

01:00:50.460 --> 01:00:51.960
I guess I was
wondering if you could

01:00:51.960 --> 01:00:54.360
take a picture of [INAUDIBLE].

01:00:56.720 --> 01:00:57.220
Yeah.

01:00:57.220 --> 01:00:57.845
Great question.

01:00:57.845 --> 01:01:00.080
So the question
was, with Parcae,

01:01:00.080 --> 01:01:01.640
are we training from scratch?

01:01:01.640 --> 01:01:04.560
Is there anything you can
do with pre-trained models?

01:01:04.560 --> 01:01:08.010
So there was a really troll
blog post from someone

01:01:08.010 --> 01:01:09.760
a few months ago where
it was like, hey, I

01:01:09.760 --> 01:01:13.740
won some leaderboard competition
without training a single thing.

01:01:13.740 --> 01:01:17.160
And what he did was he
actually looped a two or three

01:01:17.160 --> 01:01:20.880
layers in a Quinn
model, and just

01:01:20.880 --> 01:01:25.240
saw that on some math things it
started having higher quality.

01:01:25.240 --> 01:01:27.080
So we have a little
bit of work trying

01:01:27.080 --> 01:01:29.600
to look into this that
maybe coming out soon.

01:01:29.600 --> 01:01:31.400
But there could be
some models where

01:01:31.400 --> 01:01:33.520
if you do a little
bit of looping just

01:01:33.520 --> 01:01:37.200
to the pre-trained model, you
can get a higher quality thing,

01:01:37.200 --> 01:01:38.260
which is really weird.

01:01:38.260 --> 01:01:39.620
It disturbs me.

01:01:39.620 --> 01:01:42.880
I don't know why that
would possibly be the case.

01:01:42.880 --> 01:01:44.568
But yeah, we'll be--

01:01:44.568 --> 01:01:45.860
we're quite interested in this.

01:01:45.860 --> 01:01:48.800
I think we'll be looking
at it and hopefully,

01:01:48.800 --> 01:01:50.480
if I can convince
Hayden, he'll be

01:01:50.480 --> 01:01:54.640
staring at the activations--
at the actual weights

01:01:54.640 --> 01:01:58.450
to figure out why when you
loop it, it gets better.

01:01:58.450 --> 01:01:59.950
Here's just a follow up on that.

01:01:59.950 --> 01:02:03.670
So you talked about compute
optimality for the loop models,

01:02:03.670 --> 01:02:06.050
can you speak about the
inference implications

01:02:06.050 --> 01:02:09.590
and the memory and how that
can make you go faster?

01:02:09.590 --> 01:02:10.090
Yeah.

01:02:10.090 --> 01:02:11.882
So one of the reasons
that I was personally

01:02:11.882 --> 01:02:14.010
very excited in
these loop things

01:02:14.010 --> 01:02:16.702
is that, one of
the big bottlenecks

01:02:16.702 --> 01:02:18.410
to serving inference
efficiently actually

01:02:18.410 --> 01:02:20.010
ends up being GPU memory.

01:02:20.010 --> 01:02:22.050
So if you have fewer
parameters, you

01:02:22.050 --> 01:02:24.930
can fit, for example,
more KV Cache,

01:02:24.930 --> 01:02:27.930
or you can do less
communication because you

01:02:27.930 --> 01:02:31.390
need to split your model against
fewer GPUs and things like that.

01:02:31.390 --> 01:02:33.730
So there's actually
a lot of flexibility

01:02:33.730 --> 01:02:36.810
that a smaller
model will get you.

01:02:36.810 --> 01:02:38.370
I also had this
dream that if you

01:02:38.370 --> 01:02:41.130
could make the recurrent
block small enough,

01:02:41.130 --> 01:02:42.970
you could actually write
a little Megakernel

01:02:42.970 --> 01:02:45.450
to just do that recurrent
in a little very

01:02:45.450 --> 01:02:47.405
fast Megakernel loop.

01:02:47.405 --> 01:02:50.030
So far, we haven't been able to
make those blocks small enough.

01:02:50.030 --> 01:02:52.930
But I think it's
quite interesting.

01:02:52.930 --> 01:02:55.470
Certainly, with the next
generation of the LPU,

01:02:55.470 --> 01:02:57.960
the Grok chips that are
coming with NVIDIA things.

01:02:57.960 --> 01:03:02.120
Those have 250 megabytes of
memory or something like that.

01:03:02.120 --> 01:03:04.340
So the thing that you'll
be able to fit into them

01:03:04.340 --> 01:03:05.532
is very, very small.

01:03:05.532 --> 01:03:07.740
But maybe you can design
something that will actually

01:03:07.740 --> 01:03:11.500
fit into them, and then you can
just keep your weights in memory

01:03:11.500 --> 01:03:13.740
the whole time and just run
your activations through

01:03:13.740 --> 01:03:14.800
as quickly as you can.

01:03:14.800 --> 01:03:17.100
So there are non-linear
benefits that you

01:03:17.100 --> 01:03:20.300
can get if you can cross
some of these thresholds

01:03:20.300 --> 01:03:22.920
that I'm hoping that we'll be
able to get to pretty soon.

01:03:25.940 --> 01:03:27.140
Yeah, OK.

01:03:27.140 --> 01:03:30.560
[INAUDIBLE] I'm curious.

01:03:30.560 --> 01:03:33.980
I guess the trade offs
are that it's always

01:03:33.980 --> 01:03:35.660
strictly more optimal.

01:03:35.660 --> 01:03:37.280
[INAUDIBLE]

01:03:37.280 --> 01:03:37.780
Great.

01:03:37.780 --> 01:03:40.620
So the question is, what are
the trade offs for my kernels?

01:03:40.620 --> 01:03:44.020
The trade offs are people's
blood, sweat, and tears.

01:03:44.020 --> 01:03:46.780
So Megakernels, turns out
they're very, very labor

01:03:46.780 --> 01:03:48.580
intensive to write.

01:03:48.580 --> 01:03:57.540
So to give you some context, a
full talented kernel engineer

01:03:57.540 --> 01:03:59.460
over the course of a
year, will probably

01:03:59.460 --> 01:04:03.190
be able to write Megakernels
for one hardware for two

01:04:03.190 --> 01:04:06.900
or three models for
batch sizes 1 to 16.

01:04:06.900 --> 01:04:09.900
Go batch size 17, he was
like nope, start over.

01:04:09.900 --> 01:04:11.288
Got to go again.

01:04:11.288 --> 01:04:13.080
So they're very, very
challenging to write.

01:04:13.080 --> 01:04:14.830
And together, we're
trying to put together

01:04:14.830 --> 01:04:17.940
some compilers that can
automate some of that process.

01:04:17.940 --> 01:04:21.800
But I'd say, it's a very
challenging thing to do.

01:04:21.800 --> 01:04:24.140
I think the basic
Megakernel idea

01:04:24.140 --> 01:04:29.780
has gone in peaks and troughs
over the last few decades

01:04:29.780 --> 01:04:31.940
when it comes to
GPU programming.

01:04:31.940 --> 01:04:34.680
But yeah, so if you can do
it, it will go super fast.

01:04:34.680 --> 01:04:37.380
You will never be
able to go faster,

01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:41.000
but it just takes a lot of
energy and a lot of effort.

01:04:44.500 --> 01:04:45.860
Cool.

01:04:45.860 --> 01:04:47.380
Maybe I can ask a question.

01:04:47.380 --> 01:04:50.860
So can we talk a little
bit more about co-design?

01:04:50.860 --> 01:04:54.750
And in particular, you
mentioned all these new hardware

01:04:54.750 --> 01:04:57.190
like Grok and Cerebras
on the inference side.

01:04:57.190 --> 01:05:00.110
So if you're designing
a model and that's

01:05:00.110 --> 01:05:01.570
going to be the
serving platform,

01:05:01.570 --> 01:05:03.690
how should you be changing
your architecture?

01:05:03.690 --> 01:05:06.530
Yeah, I think there's
a couple of things

01:05:06.530 --> 01:05:07.530
that you should look at.

01:05:07.530 --> 01:05:11.310
So one is, you are going to
be most constrained by memory

01:05:11.310 --> 01:05:11.990
first.

01:05:11.990 --> 01:05:14.510
So if you know that you're
going to be taking a model

01:05:14.510 --> 01:05:19.160
and serving it a
particular Cerebras chip,

01:05:19.160 --> 01:05:20.910
you want to go look
at the Cerebras wafer,

01:05:20.910 --> 01:05:23.190
and figure out how much
memory you have, and then

01:05:23.190 --> 01:05:26.390
size your model so that it can
fit there with enough KV Cache

01:05:26.390 --> 01:05:29.310
or whatever to spare.

01:05:29.310 --> 01:05:31.750
If you look at carefully,
at the Chinese models that

01:05:31.750 --> 01:05:35.510
are coming out, they've started
making some interesting choices

01:05:35.510 --> 01:05:37.710
that suggest they
might be starting

01:05:37.710 --> 01:05:41.110
to think about the Huawei
chips that are coming out.

01:05:41.110 --> 01:05:44.690
You'll see quantization
choices that people make.

01:05:44.690 --> 01:05:46.350
So if you have a
model that you're

01:05:46.350 --> 01:05:48.530
intending to serve
on NVIDIA GPUs,

01:05:48.530 --> 01:05:51.040
for example, NVIDIA's new
Nemotron model that they

01:05:51.040 --> 01:05:54.420
released, you will train
that model in NVFP4.

01:05:54.420 --> 01:05:58.400
This is a FP4 format that is
proprietary to NVIDIA chips.

01:05:58.400 --> 01:06:01.780
If you're not going to run it
on NVIDIA chips, if you're AMD,

01:06:01.780 --> 01:06:04.960
then you're going to run this
other format called MXFP4.

01:06:04.960 --> 01:06:08.360
They each have
their pros and cons.

01:06:08.360 --> 01:06:11.360
You'll make these subtle
choices based on the hardware

01:06:11.360 --> 01:06:14.120
that you're choosing.

01:06:14.120 --> 01:06:17.360
I think there was a
question over there.

01:06:17.360 --> 01:06:20.160
I was wondering if it's
ever-- if you just care about

01:06:20.160 --> 01:06:22.720
compute optimal training,
is it ever optimal to loop

01:06:22.720 --> 01:06:24.920
as opposed to just
adding more parameters,

01:06:24.920 --> 01:06:28.920
or is it mainly like a trick
to reduce inference cost?

01:06:28.920 --> 01:06:30.040
Yeah.

01:06:30.040 --> 01:06:31.600
So the trick with
compute optimal

01:06:31.600 --> 01:06:34.303
is it is, usually, like
given a flop budget--

01:06:34.303 --> 01:06:35.220
--repeat the question.

01:06:35.220 --> 01:06:35.520
Oh yeah.

01:06:35.520 --> 01:06:35.700
Sorry.

01:06:35.700 --> 01:06:36.200
Sorry.

01:06:36.200 --> 01:06:38.040
Yeah, the question
was for Parcae,

01:06:38.040 --> 01:06:39.600
would you ever choose
to loop instead

01:06:39.600 --> 01:06:41.880
of increasing parameters?

01:06:41.880 --> 01:06:43.920
And so the trick
with compute optimal

01:06:43.920 --> 01:06:46.100
is always given
some flop budget,

01:06:46.100 --> 01:06:48.880
figure out what you want to hit.

01:06:48.880 --> 01:06:51.260
It's almost a little bit
contrived in that sense,

01:06:51.260 --> 01:06:54.180
because if you want a
higher quality model,

01:06:54.180 --> 01:06:56.100
you should just increase
your FLOPs budget.

01:06:56.100 --> 01:06:59.400
And if you decided on your
model size, just train longer.

01:06:59.400 --> 01:07:04.680
Or if you're restricted by your
model size, then loop longer.

01:07:04.680 --> 01:07:07.960
Or if you run out of data,
then pick the model size

01:07:07.960 --> 01:07:11.560
that you think will
be as overtrained-- as

01:07:11.560 --> 01:07:14.520
well trained as you
can for that size.

01:07:14.520 --> 01:07:17.200
So I think there are choices.

01:07:17.200 --> 01:07:20.480
And of course, you make all
these choices in the context of,

01:07:20.480 --> 01:07:22.780
do I think it'll get picked up?

01:07:22.780 --> 01:07:24.348
How am I going to serve it?

01:07:24.348 --> 01:07:26.140
If you're going to
release the open source,

01:07:26.140 --> 01:07:29.480
what is the size of model
that people can serve

01:07:29.480 --> 01:07:31.120
like on your laptop today?

01:07:31.120 --> 01:07:34.180
So I think all these choices
go into making a choice.

01:07:34.180 --> 01:07:37.280
All these things go into making
a choice of how big a model

01:07:37.280 --> 01:07:39.612
you choose to train.

01:07:39.612 --> 01:07:41.320
I think if you just
make the model bigger

01:07:41.320 --> 01:07:44.520
and train it on more data, it's
always going to get better.

01:07:44.520 --> 01:07:51.230
But I think it really depends
on those design points.

01:07:54.970 --> 01:07:56.955
Maybe another question
about co-design.

01:07:56.955 --> 01:07:58.330
So you mentioned
at the beginning

01:07:58.330 --> 01:08:03.290
different use cases like agentic
coding versus let's say batch

01:08:03.290 --> 01:08:05.150
processing of data.

01:08:05.150 --> 01:08:09.530
So what are the most
dramatic differences

01:08:09.530 --> 01:08:12.050
in terms of optimal
architectures

01:08:12.050 --> 01:08:14.470
that you see across
different use cases?

01:08:14.470 --> 01:08:16.770
And at the end of
the day, of course

01:08:16.770 --> 01:08:20.569
model developers has to
pick one and try to not be

01:08:20.569 --> 01:08:23.010
reasonable on many use cases.

01:08:23.010 --> 01:08:24.010
Yeah.

01:08:24.010 --> 01:08:25.109
That's a great question.

01:08:25.109 --> 01:08:28.790
So I think one of
the big differences--

01:08:28.790 --> 01:08:31.750
so when you have these
agentic looped workflows,

01:08:31.750 --> 01:08:33.930
one of the things
that matters a lot

01:08:33.930 --> 01:08:37.590
is that you want to keep your
KV Cache as hot as possible.

01:08:37.590 --> 01:08:40.210
So if you're doing a big batch
processing thing where you only

01:08:40.210 --> 01:08:42.990
see each document once
and then you translate it,

01:08:42.990 --> 01:08:47.220
the KV cache doesn't
necessarily matter as much.

01:08:47.220 --> 01:08:50.240
If you look at something like
the DeepSeek MLA attention,

01:08:50.240 --> 01:08:52.700
that is a radical
compression of the KV

01:08:52.700 --> 01:08:56.899
cache compared to what you
would have for another model.

01:08:56.899 --> 01:09:01.420
Or if you have the model that
can process its KV Cache in FPA

01:09:01.420 --> 01:09:04.140
or FP4, those are
pretty big departures

01:09:04.140 --> 01:09:06.500
in terms of the
size of the KV Cache

01:09:06.500 --> 01:09:08.939
that if you are sensitive
to an agentic workflow

01:09:08.939 --> 01:09:10.859
you would look at.

01:09:10.859 --> 01:09:13.740
Then of course, the biggest
one is like causal attention

01:09:13.740 --> 01:09:15.700
or non-causal attention.

01:09:15.700 --> 01:09:18.365
So if you're just doing a big
batch processing workflows.

01:09:18.365 --> 01:09:19.740
Like for the
longest time, Google

01:09:19.740 --> 01:09:21.939
was just using Bert models.

01:09:21.939 --> 01:09:25.580
I think probably still
uses Bert models on search.

01:09:25.580 --> 01:09:27.385
And that's because
you don't really

01:09:27.385 --> 01:09:29.760
need to be generating a bunch
of tokens on the other end.

01:09:29.760 --> 01:09:33.460
So you just do that big
bidirectional attention once,

01:09:33.460 --> 01:09:35.140
you get your vector
out, then you

01:09:35.140 --> 01:09:38.380
stick that in database, do
whatever you will with it.

01:09:38.380 --> 01:09:43.040
Whereas the chat workflows,
those processing,

01:09:43.040 --> 01:09:46.437
there's always going to be
this decode portion of it.

01:09:46.437 --> 01:09:48.229
I think there have been
intermediate things

01:09:48.229 --> 01:09:53.510
like T5 was at some point
a choice that people made

01:09:53.510 --> 01:09:56.950
to do some bidirectional
processing, and then also

01:09:56.950 --> 01:09:59.710
some generation.

01:09:59.710 --> 01:10:03.510
OK, maybe one last question.

01:10:03.510 --> 01:10:04.210
Oh, yeah.

01:10:04.210 --> 01:10:09.510
[INAUDIBLE], it was
about using everything

01:10:09.510 --> 01:10:12.710
into one kernel,
[INAUDIBLE] but what

01:10:12.710 --> 01:10:15.190
you were talking about before.

01:10:15.190 --> 01:10:18.950
So I was just wondering,
how does that [INAUDIBLE]

01:10:18.950 --> 01:10:21.870
work a communication
across multiple computers

01:10:21.870 --> 01:10:26.270
are scaling the cost
of [INAUDIBLE] that.

01:10:26.270 --> 01:10:27.170
Yeah, great question.

01:10:27.170 --> 01:10:29.510
So the question is about,
how does Megakernel

01:10:29.510 --> 01:10:34.590
work when you have multiple
GPUs communication in the loop?

01:10:34.590 --> 01:10:38.010
We had some very early
preliminary work about this.

01:10:38.010 --> 01:10:40.670
So it turns out you
can also fuse the NCCL

01:10:40.670 --> 01:10:44.270
calls into the Megakernel
if you set it up correctly.

01:10:44.270 --> 01:10:46.710
I think we haven't found
a really great killer use

01:10:46.710 --> 01:10:50.350
case for that yet,
where sometimes you're

01:10:50.350 --> 01:10:53.190
just bound by the latency
of the NCCL call itself.

01:10:53.190 --> 01:10:57.070
So these are also pieces of
things that you can fuse.

01:10:57.070 --> 01:11:00.790
When DeepSeek 4 came out,
the DeepSeek 4s released

01:11:00.790 --> 01:11:04.130
a Megakernel for the mixture of
experts inference layer that can

01:11:04.130 --> 01:11:06.630
run just for that where they
actually did fuse some of those

01:11:06.630 --> 01:11:08.230
communication.

01:11:08.230 --> 01:11:10.090
So I think what we
are starting to see,

01:11:10.090 --> 01:11:12.670
we're starting to
see more of is,

01:11:12.670 --> 01:11:14.250
as you get to more
of these models,

01:11:14.250 --> 01:11:18.830
you'll have a little Megakernel
for a part for the computation,

01:11:18.830 --> 01:11:22.252
but you won't necessarily have a
Megakernel for the whole model,

01:11:22.252 --> 01:11:24.710
unless of course, you pay the
blood, sweat, and tears price

01:11:24.710 --> 01:11:28.790
and then really, really
get the whole thing going.

01:11:28.790 --> 01:11:30.570
OK, I think that's
all the time we have.

01:11:30.570 --> 01:11:31.810
Let's thank, Dan again.

01:11:31.810 --> 01:11:33.390
[APPLAUSE]

01:11:33.390 --> 01:11:35.860
Thanks so much for having me.

